---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../../data/site-config';
import { src } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { VALUE } from '../../content/home';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Flood insurance data publisher: who runs Flood Cost ZIP';
const description = 'Flood Cost ZIP is published by Radif Partners from FEMA and OpenFEMA files: medians by ZIP code and county, no policy sold, no agent, no details asked.';
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-serif">
    <h1>About Flood Cost ZIP</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>The gap this site fills</h2>
    <p>Pages that answer "how much is flood insurance" usually give one national number: an average from a survey, a figure from an old FEMA fact sheet, or a quote engine that wants your address and phone number first. None of them tells a homeowner in a given ZIP code what the neighbors actually pay, or how many more years of increases the law still allows. The federal flood program publishes the files that answer both questions; they are large and technical, and nobody had turned them into a lookup.</p>
    <p>{VALUE}</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The site is published by {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds calculators on public rules and public data. It is not an insurer, an insurance agency, a broker or a lead generator. It is not affiliated with FEMA, the National Flood Insurance Program or any government body, receives no commission and has no partner to send you to. Pages carry no affiliate link.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>What we rely on</h2>
    <p>FEMA's single-family cost tables (<Fragment set:html={src('fema_sfh_cost', 'fema.gov')} />), the OpenFEMA policy and claims datasets (<Fragment set:html={src('openfema_policies', 'policies')} />, <Fragment set:html={src('openfema_claims', 'claims')} />), the National Flood Insurance Act as codified in 42 U.S.C. 4011 and following, the regulations of 44 CFR Parts 61 and 64, and FEMA's Flood Insurance Manual of October 2025. This product uses the Federal Emergency Management Agency's OpenFEMA API, but is not endorsed by FEMA. The Federal Government or FEMA cannot vouch for the data or analyses derived from these data after the data have been retrieved from the Agency's website(s).</p>
    <h2>What the site leaves out</h2>
    <p>It does not quote a premium for your building: only an insurer or an agent can, from the building's own characteristics. Medians describe the policies in an area, not your house. Commercial buildings, condominium associations and two-to-four family buildings are outside the single-family figures. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method page</a> lists every step and its limits; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains corrections.</p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, for corrections and sources. We answer questions about a rule or a figure, not about your own policy.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
